belgian-azerty-windows (0.9.9) noble; urgency=medium

  * Shift+AltGr on the ù, µ and = keys gives the same accent as AltGr
    (´ ` ~), as on Windows, with the Windows profile (new option
    be_azerty:accents; setting ACCENTS=windows|linux). The Linux profile
    keeps caron, breve and dot above (ǎ ă ȧ).
  * Accent then space as on Windows, for users who switch it on:
    "be-azerty-fix accent-space on" writes a small ~/.XCompose (´ then
    space gives ´, ¨ then space gives ¨). Never changes a ~/.XCompose of
    the user's own; uninstall removes only an unchanged one.
  * Belgian Apple keyboards, opt-in: APPLE=yes gives the characters
    printed on a Belgian Apple keyboard (option be_azerty:mac, generated
    from Unicode CLDR 43). "be-azerty-fix apple-iso-keys on|off|auto" sets
    the kernel's hid_apple iso_layout for keyboards whose key left of 1
    and key next to left Shift come out swapped. Not tested on a real
    Apple keyboard.
  * Updating from 0.9.8 keeps the accents you had (caron, breve, dot
    above), also with the Windows profile; "sudo be-azerty-fix system
    ACCENTS=windows" gives the Windows accents. New installs with the
    Windows profile get them by default.
  * APPLE=yes is refused when no Apple keyboard is connected (it would
    change every keyboard and the login screen); FORCE=1 overrides. The
    text console and disk unlock keep the PC layout.
  * The new options only change the Belgian layout, wherever it sits in
    the layout list (be,us or us,be), and only the standard Belgian
    variant (not nodeadkeys, oss, iso-alternate, wang).
  * accent-space refuses to switch on when a "US international"-type
    layout is in use (its own accents would change); FORCE=1 overrides.
  * Uninstall and removing the package take all of this project's
    keyboard options out of every setting (system default, every user's
    GNOME and Cinnamon options), and remove the Apple key setting.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:00:00 +0000

belgian-azerty-windows (0.9.8) noble; urgency=medium

  * Other layouts and input methods are kept. Up to 0.9.7, "be-azerty-fix
    system" (also run by the Period/Comma choice in the "Belgian keyboard"
    window) and "be-azerty-fix live" replaced the desktop's layout list
    with Belgian only, removing a second layout or a Chinese/Japanese
    input method. Now Belgian is put first and every other entry stays, in
    the GNOME, Cinnamon and MATE lists, the running X session and
    /etc/default/keyboard. A list that cannot be read is left unchanged.
  * The ibus skip for the Terminal, the text editor and VS Code is off
    unless the user switches it on (tick box or "ibus-skip auto on"). Up
    to 0.9.7 it was on by default with the Windows profile, although no
    swaps were measured at normal typing speed. Users who never chose lose
    the skip at their next login.
  * keyd now comes from the keyd-team PPA (or the distribution from Ubuntu
    25.04). This project no longer ships its own keyd build or its extra
    keyd patch (now only a proposal for keyd upstream). New command
    "be-azerty-fix switch-keyd" replaces the keyd build of 0.9.7 and earlier by
    the PPA's; "check" and "system" point to it. Uninstall only removes
    keyd when it is that old build.
  * The boot image is rebuilt only when /etc/default/keyboard changed (or
    is newer than the boot image).
  * Uninstall and removing the package take the Caps Lock option out of
    every user's own settings, not only the user who runs it.
  * "Belgian keyboard" window: wider, so the German column header fits;
    the fast-typing text says to tick it only when swaps are seen.
  * Autostart entry text says what it does.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 23:00:00 +0000

